{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game character design game character design","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md","fileName":"Game Character Design.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Game Character Design","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 15:28\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Game Presentation]] · [[Character and Interactive Story Design]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Presentation","Character and Interactive Story Design","Game Part","Player-Avatar Relationship","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","fundamentalsofgamedesign3e.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game character design game character design","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md","fileName":"Game Character Design.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Game Character Design","heading":"Game Character Design","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Game character design uses visible form to distinguish characters and communicate the rules that govern them. Shape, color, equipment, posture, and motion can suggest threat, role, ability, or relationship before the player acts.\n\nIn fast play, the player must recognize important differences at a glance. Distinct designs therefore support both narrative characterization and the reliable identification of [[Game Part|game objects]] with different behaviors.\n\nCharacter design also coordinates the intended [[Player-Avatar Relationship]] with model sheets, movesets, back story, behavior, and voice. A compelling character belongs in the game world, supports the required actions, remains memorable, and avoids relying on a stereotype in place of depth.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Presentation","Character and Interactive Story Design","Game Part","Player-Avatar Relationship","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","fundamentalsofgamedesign3e.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"game character design game character design","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Game Character Design.md","fileName":"Game Character Design.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Game Character Design","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[agamedesignvocabulary.pdf]]\n\n[[fundamentalsofgamedesign3e.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Game Presentation","Character and Interactive Story Design","Game Part","Player-Avatar Relationship","agamedesignvocabulary.pdf","fundamentalsofgamedesign3e.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}